Types of Constructors in Java:
A constructor in Java is a special member of a class used to initialize objects.
- Constructor name must be the same as the class name.
- It does not have a return type.
- It is automatically called when an object is created.
1. Default Constructor:
      A constructor automatically provided by the Java compiler when we do not write any constructor in the class.
   ex:
     class Student {
    int id;

    void display() {
        System.out.println(id);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s = new Student();
        s.display();
    }
}
2. No-Argument Constructor:
   A constructor explicitly written by the programmer without parameters.
    ex:class Student {
    Student() {
        System.out.println("No Argument Constructor");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s = new Student();
    }
}
3. Parameterized Constructor:
    A constructor that accepts parameters to initialize object values.
    ex:
  class Student {
    int id;
    String name;

    Student(int a, String b) {
        id = a;
        name = b;
    }

    void display() {
        System.out.println(id);
        System.out.println(name);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Student s = new Student(101, "Veeru");
        s.display();
    }
}

Access Modifiers in Java:
   Access Modifiers are keywords in Java that define the accessibility (visibility) of classes, variables, methods and constructors.
1. public:
-->Accessible from anywhere.
-->Can be accessed within the same class, same package and different packages.
 ex: 
   class Student {
    public int id = 101;

    public void display() {
        System.out.println(id);
    }
}
2. private:
-->Accessible only within the same class.
-->Cannot be accessed directly outside the class.
ex:
   class Student {
    private int id = 101;

    public void display() {
        System.out.println(id);
    }
}
3. protected:
--> Accessible within the same package.
--> Also accessible in subclasses of different packages through inheritance.
ex :
class Student {
    protected int id = 101;
}
4. default (no modifier):
--> When we don't specify any access modifier, it is called default access.
--> Accessible only within the same package.
ex:
class Student {
    int id = 101;

    void display() {
        System.out.println(id);
    }
}


